  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "b1",
      "narration": "Twice a day, the sea creeps up the beach, then slides back again. Nobody pushes it. This rise and fall is the tide, and it happens because the Moon is pulling on our oceans from far away. Let's see how that works.",
      "ops": [
        {
          "id": "o1",
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How Tides Work",
          "level": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "o2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The sea rises and falls twice a day, and the Moon is the main reason why."
        },
        {
          "id": "o3",
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Big idea: gravity from the Moon and Sun shapes the oceans.",
          "tone": "key"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b2",
      "narration": "Imagine the Earth as a blue ball, with a big bulge of water pulled toward the Moon. On the side facing the Moon, water is pulled most strongly. On the far side, the Moon pulls the Earth away from the water, so it bulges too.",
      "ops": [
        {
          "id": "o4",
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Bulges of Water",
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "id": "o5",
          "type": "figure",
          "frame": {
            "width": 640,
            "height": 340
          },
          "parts": [
            {
              "name": "earth",
              "cue": 4,
              "marks": [
                {
                  "kind": "circle",
                  "cx": 320,
                  "cy": 170,
                  "r": 60,
                  "color": "blue",
                  "fill": "#cfe6ff"
                },
                {
